Assignment Description & Context

Scenario:
Create a mortality prediction model as a decision-support tool for ICU physicians.
Current practice uses the APACHE scoring system (APACHE II, III, and IV), but these rely on linear assumptions and lack thorough validation across diverse populations.
Data Source:
Data collected from MIT’s GOSSIS initiative, comprising over 130,000 ICU visits over one year.
Access Steps:
Register at https://physionet.org
Sign the data use agreement
Download the dataset (ensure local, secure storage to protect patient data).
Project Tasks & Structure

Problem Understanding (20 points):

Problem Background: Define the clinical challenge and the intended benefits.
Risk Assessment:
Identify all stakeholders.
Analyze benefits and risks (including harm, affected stakeholders, moral values, severity, and likelihood).
Summarize risk mitigation strategies.
Requirements & Success Criteria: List main project requirements and technical as well as real-world success metrics.
Task Formulation: Translate the clinical problem into a machine learning task (input data, target variable, and evaluation metrics).
Assumptions: Document assumptions and how they might be validated.
Data Understanding & Preparation (20 points):

Data Exploration: Investigate the dataset for missing values, distributions, correlations, etc.
Pre-processing: Develop and describe a pipeline for cleaning and preparing the data.
Data Sheet: Create a detailed datasheet (appendix) based on guidelines (e.g., Gebru et al. [2018]).
Modelling (20 points):

Candidate Algorithms: Consider models such as logistic regression, decision trees, random forests, and XGBoost.
Model Selection Pipeline: Outline the hyperparameter tuning, cross-validation, and evaluation metrics used during model selection.
Results: Present and interpret outcomes from model training and selection.
Evaluation (20 points):

Quantitative Evaluation:
Evaluate the final model on a test set.
Compare performance with the APACHE IV scoring system.
Qualitative Evaluation:
Use interpretability techniques (e.g., post-hoc explanations) to assess feature importance and model behavior.
Model Card: Develop a model card summarizing key aspects of the final model.
Conclusion and Discussion (10 points):

Recap: Summarize key accomplishments and results.
Limitations: Discuss the limitations, including ethical considerations.
Future Work: Suggest next steps for evaluation and deployment.
Recommendations: Advise on conditions under which the model might be used in practice.
Individual Reflection & Work Breakdown:

Self-Assessment: Each group member provides a brief self-assessment and reflection (100–200 words).
Work Breakdown: Detail the contribution of each team member and the approximate hours invested.
Tools, Resources, and References

Programming & Libraries:
Data pre-processing: numpy, pandas, scikit-learn, polars
Visualization: matplotlib, seaborn
Machine learning: scikit-learn, xgboost
Fairness and interpretability: fairlearn, AIF360, interpretml, shap, AIX360
Version Control: GitHub (or similar) for managing project files.
Suggested Readings:
Literature on APACHE IV and mortality assessment.
Studies on algorithmic bias and the importance of interpretability (e.g., papers by Obermeyer et al. and Rudin).
Additional guidelines on data sheets for datasets and model cards.